From 269a715946bbe04478568f8c7f5db693e2876eaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>

Date: Tue, 2 Sep 2025 18:59:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [plan_cache][asan]using the ASan pkg to
 start a DStore instance, find another new coredump scenerio including CTE
 query. (the coredump won't occur while using the release pkg)

Cause of the bug
================

We have a prepared statement with CTEs and a subquery in one CTE.

First execution:

we optimize CTE cte1. There we have bool_col<>'2020', bool_col is
tinyint, so constant folding (fold_condition()) recognizes that this
condition is always true except if bool_col is NULL, so replaces the
'<>' with 'if((`test`.`tbl_1_all_type2index_desc`.`bool_col` is
null),NULL,1)'. This pushes an "Item change" into
THD::change_list (fold_or_simplify() calling change_item_tree()). So
when we come to deciding if we can cache the plan for subquery #4 (the
ANY in cte2, select_number==4), we hit:

  if (unit->outer_query_block()) {
    ...
    // If Item tree changes have been done, given that THD::change_list is
    // common to all query blocks, this makes it impossible for us to identify
    // which changes must be replayed later in apply_cached_plan().
    if (!thd->change_list.is_empty()) return false;

and decide to not cache the plan of subquery #4.

Second execution:

we optimize CTE cte1. There we have bool_col<>'NULL', which means
bool_col<>0 (conversion of string to number yields 0), constant
folding cannot simplify that, so records no Item change. So later we
decide that we can cache the plan for subquery #4.

Third execution:

we optimize CTE cte1. There we have bool_col<>'1901', so it's like in
the first execution: we push an Item change. When we come to
optimizing subquery #4, we see there's a cached plan, and expect no
Item change to exist:

  if (!thd->change_list.is_empty()) {
    // We expect the change list to be empty, because we are in execution,
    // resolution has not been re-done, and we are at the very start of
    // optimization. If not empty, we would not know how to undo (in case of
    // error) only the changes which apply_cached_plan() is about to do.
    assert(false);
    return true;
  }

but there are Item changes actually. The comment is wrong because it
assumes there is a single query block (it is true in that case). We
are not at the very start of optimization as cte1 has been optimized
before subquery #4 which we're looking at now, and has pushed Item
changes onto THD's list.

This bug is specific of queries which use subqueries, and affects only
debug binaries (in a release binary we properly just 'return true' and
do not apply this plan).

Fix
===

(1) remove the assertion.

(2) Also, for extra safety, move the No-Item-change check to
apply_cached_plan_if_suitable(), so that when this check fails it
explicitly invalidates the plan. This ensures that the invalidation
counter is incremented, helping to control memory usage (grep for
PLAN_CACHE_MAX_INVALIDATIONS).

(3) Also noticed that one enum value was unused (even in the original
impl in MySQL) so removed it.

(4) Noticed a std::set was passed by value, for efficiency let's pass
it by ref-to-const.

(5) A comment; and a compiler warning for unused variable.
